00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.210
of these things, the pros and
cons, and that kind of stuff.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.220
And then we'll talk
about, what does a journey

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.480
to a modern application
look like today?

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:51.240
So let's start with what.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.910
So, Shahid, what is a container?

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.410
SHAHID MASUD: So
yes, so containers,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.230
you hear a lot of buzzwords
associated with containers,

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.430
that they're lightweight,
portable, fast,

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:01.780
and so on and so forth.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.130
So let's look at, what
does this really mean?

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:10.050
So containers in themselves are
really about two technologies.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:13.230
First, it's a way of packaging
your application and all

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.520
of its dependencies
into a single image.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.730
And then the second
capability is the ability

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:25.450
to run that image in an
isolated set of processes.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.930
So those are sort of
really the two main things

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.370
that containers provide you.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.460
They let you package everything
that your application needs

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.250
into one single image,
and then provide you

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.290
a mechanism to run that
image in an isolated manner.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.590
So that's really what this is.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.750
And the key term
here is process.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.070
So containers run as processes.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.880
So just like when you start
something on your computer,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.710
it starts a process
in the background.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.110
It runs as a process.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:52.650
Containers run the same way.

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.420
And containers are
a group of processes

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.390
that sort of work together
to provide some functionality

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.200
that you need.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.740
JAMES DUKE: So who here has
built and deployed a container?

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:02.580
Show of hands.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.325
OK, not bad.

00:03:03.325 --> 00:03:05.540
Looks like about
half, I would say.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.010
Who's used some kind of
container orchestration system,

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.150
Kubernetes, [INAUDIBLE]?

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.290
OK, about a third.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:11.970
OK, that's good.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:12.470
That's good.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.430
That helps us with
the level setting

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.760
where we go through this.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:16.740
So for those of you
who've deployed it,

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.157
you'll be very
familiar with this.

00:03:18.157 --> 00:03:20.060
It gives you this
nice packaging.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.060
You get the application.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.103
You get the dependencies.

00:03:22.103 --> 00:03:23.770
For those who are
less familiar with it,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.150
this may be a new concept.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.640
But the idea is that now I
only depend on an environment

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.200
to execute the container.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.840
I don't have to care about
bringing in libraries.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.007
I don't have to care about
bringing in dependencies,

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:39.140
because I've packaged them
all up into my container.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.230
SHAHID MASUD: So
let's look at how

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.560
containers and some other ways
of running your application

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.860
sort of compare.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.740
Some of you might be
familiar with this kind

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.550
of a diagram, which shows
what running applications

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.970
on a shared host looks
like, and that's basically,

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.450
the host provides the kernel and
it provides also the libraries

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.700
that your applications
need, and then

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.340
all of the applications that
are running on that host

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.820
share that kernel
and the libraries.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.680
The challenge with
this kind of setup

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.320
is since the
libraries are shared,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.780
you have to be really careful
which version of which

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:15.940
library is installed on that VM.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.220
And for that reason,
a lot of the time,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.790
people actually run one
application per host.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.220
So they run one application.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.470
They bring all the
dependencies that they need.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.080
They bring it to that host.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.470
Configure the host to be the
right kind of environment

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.660
for that application,
and then run it there.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.250
JAMES DUKE: In many ways,
that's very convenient, right?

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:35.620
Because we now have a
kind of a logical grouping

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.410
of our applications.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.735
The problem is, there's
kind of minimal boundaries

00:04:41.735 --> 00:04:42.860
between those applications.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.443
You can do some stuff
with sandboxing.

00:04:44.443 --> 00:04:47.560
But generally speaking, in
this model, those applications,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.480
they're all in a shared space.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.550
And so I don't
have any security--

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.680
minimal security boundaries
between those things.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:58.160
So that doesn't help me when
those applications should

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.840
be isolated from one another.

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:02.660
So if we go up the
stack a little bit,

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.580
the next thing we might
think about is VMs.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:06.770
Well, why don't we take
those applications,

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:12.020
those dependencies, and then
we can fit multiple single host

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.690
application servers onto
one physical machine.

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:17.620
People talk about
bin packing, right?

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.810
We're now more effectively
using more of that machine's

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.050
resources, because we
have more things running

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.003
than we were able to before.

00:05:26.003 --> 00:05:27.420
But the trade-off
here, of course,

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.860
is the libraries are not common,
and not just the libraries,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.000
the kernel.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.520
So now in a given host,
given physical machine,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.060
I'm running multiple kernels.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:39.950
And there is an overhead
for having done that.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:43.280
Each kernel there will use
its own set of resources, CPU,

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.050
memory, and its boot
up time is a factor.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.150
Every time I boot
a kernel, there

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.700
is a time penalty in doing so.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:53.750
Especially [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.070
Modern kernels can
boot very quickly,

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.600
but there's still a penalty
there, because you still

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:57.830
have to do it there.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.260
SHAHID MASUD: It can take a
matter of minutes for a VM

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:01.220
to boot up.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:04.370
And it does provide a lot of
isolation, as we were saying,

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.550
but the cost is that
it's not very flexible.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.170
If you need to
resize a VM, if you

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.060
need to give it
more resources, you

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.280
have to really essentially
rebuild that VM.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:17.060
So that's where people went
to container technology.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.030
Google was one of the
pioneers in that technology.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.760
All of our workloads
internally run as containers,

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:23.910
predominantly.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.310
So the containers provide
isolation within one host.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:31.520
So instead of breaking up
that host into individual VMs,

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.200
we use some of the
mechanisms that

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.870
are available in the
kernel, in the Linux kernel,

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:40.030
to isolate a set of processes
from another set of processes.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:42.110
And each of those
set is a container.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.660
The great thing is that
they share the same kernel.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.720
So once the host has booted
up, the kernel has loaded,

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.900
there's no more delay in
launching these containers.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.460
And starting each
of these containers

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:53.690
is like running a process.

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:57.090
As soon as you type in a
command on your laptop,

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.360
for example, the application
comes up quickly.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.750
It doesn't have to wait for
your machine to boot up.

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.283
So that's some of the
advantages of using containers.

00:07:05.283 --> 00:07:07.700
JAMES DUKE: Yeah, it totally
removes that kernel boot time

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.410
that I was talking
about for the VMs.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:10.602
It uses the same kernel.

00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:11.810
The kernel's already running.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.240
You just spin up
another process.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.460
The reason we're kind of
doubling down on that is--

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.585
we'll talk in a minute
about what does cloud native

00:07:18.585 --> 00:07:20.030
computing look like?

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.542
One of the most important
parts is scalability.

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:24.000
To me, when someone
says, I'm going

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.200
to build a cloud native
application, that means

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:27.710
it's going to be very scalable.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:30.760
And VMs, they can
scale very well.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.320
But imagine a scenario,
let's say, well,

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:35.420
like an unnamed company
who may or may not

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.730
have released a new
product this morning.

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:40.790
Imagine that your press release
generated a bunch of traffic

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:43.217
to your application.

00:07:43.217 --> 00:07:45.050
You need to get a
response out very quickly.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:49.100
If you have to wait
for kernels to boot up,

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.743
even if it is only
a minute or two,

00:07:50.743 --> 00:07:52.910
that may result in a poor
experience for your users,

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.535
and that's especially concerning
if it's the first time they're

00:07:55.535 --> 00:07:56.660
experiencing your system.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.910
There's nothing worse than
sitting on a loading screen

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:00.952
the first time you ever
use a product to turn you

00:08:00.952 --> 00:08:02.360
away from using it.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.335
So with containers,
that's not a problem.

00:08:04.335 --> 00:08:05.210
If you want to spin--

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:06.865
I mean, within reason, right?

00:08:06.865 --> 00:08:08.240
It depends on how
many containers

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:09.762
you can fit on a given kernel.

00:08:09.762 --> 00:08:11.720
But within reason, it
allows you to scale much,

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:14.053
much, much faster, because
you don't have that boot time

00:08:14.053 --> 00:08:16.040
penalty.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:18.190
SHAHID MASUD: One
drawback with containers

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:21.912
is that even though there's
less dependency on the host,

00:08:21.912 --> 00:08:23.620
there's still a
dependency on the kernel.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.600
Since the kernel
is shared, you have

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:27.040
to target a particular kernel.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.560
But the good thing is,
these days, Linux kernel

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:30.590
is pretty standard.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:33.669
So a lot of applications
target the Linux kernel

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:35.940
and can run as containers.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.630
So with that in mind, let's
revisit some of those buzzwords

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:41.970
to see if you have a
better appreciation of what

00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:43.000
that means.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.350
So as we described it,
containers are lightweight.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:49.410
And it's true, because they
don't have the overhead

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.090
of creating a full VM.

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.070
So virtual machine,
when you create one,

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.730
you have to provide the host
OS image on it, libraries,

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:00.240
and everything that you need
to run your application.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.970
JAMES DUKE: And in a container,
you only put in what you need.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.210
You're able to specify just
the bare minimum set of things

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.415
that the application needs
in order to do its job.

00:09:08.415 --> 00:09:09.790
So you can end up
with containers

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.360
that are very, very, very
small from a actual disk space

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.040
perspective.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.540
SHAHID MASUD: And
they are portable,

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.840
because you're bringing all
your dependencies with you.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.660
So any libraries that you
need, you're bringing with you.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.940
And all you need on
the host is really

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.302
the kernel and some runtime
that can run your container.

00:09:27.302 --> 00:09:29.010
And those are pretty
standard these days,

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:31.590
the container run times,
and it's pretty easy

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.660
to install that on any host.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.650
So that what makes your
containers portable.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:42.210
And it's not just portable
from one VM to the other,

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.240
but it's also portable
across environments.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.120
You can run the same
container on-prem

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.260
and on any sort of
cloud provider as well.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:52.970
JAMES DUKE: Who--

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:53.720
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah, go ahead.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:55.720
JAMES DUKE: Who caught
the keynote this morning?

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.380
Anyone see the keynote speech?

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:01.730
Yeah, so you saw our glorious
leaders talking about Anthos

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.340
and what Anthos
brings to the table.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:09.960
So Anthos fundamentally builds
the construct of a container.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.780
If you have a world
where you have

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:16.230
that hybrid,
multi-cloud environment,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:18.000
once your application
is containerized,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.970
moving it between those clouds
or between those environments

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:22.595
is very, very, very
straightforward.

00:10:22.595 --> 00:10:24.720
So if you have a platform
where you can orchestrate

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:28.177
all of your security, your
networking, your monitoring

00:10:28.177 --> 00:10:29.760
with your service
mission, everything,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.135
and your configuration
management

00:10:31.135 --> 00:10:33.610
all across those
different environments,

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:35.470
the question is
not, can I move it?

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.400
The question is, where
would be the optimal place

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.017
for that particular workload
or that particular application?

00:10:41.017 --> 00:10:42.600
So when we say
portable, that's really

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.650
what we're talking about.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.493
It's not moving it
from VM to the other.

00:10:45.493 --> 00:10:46.660
It's much broader than that.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.720
It's how do I put
this application

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:50.865
so it's as close to
my user as possible?

00:10:50.865 --> 00:10:52.740
Or maybe it's something
as simple as this one

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.840
particular platform is more
cost-effective for the type

00:10:54.840 --> 00:10:56.040
of workload that we're running.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:57.457
Whatever it might
be, it gives you

00:10:57.457 --> 00:11:00.833
the power to choose to run your
application wherever you want.

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:02.000
It's this old phrase, right?

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.278
Build once, run anyway.

00:11:03.278 --> 00:11:04.070
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:05.250
Yeah.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:07.110
And then they start fast.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:10.700
So as we said, since there's
no overhead of starting a VM,

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.525
your containers
start as processes

00:11:12.525 --> 00:11:14.290
would on an ordinary host.

00:11:14.290 --> 00:11:16.680
So they boot-- they start--

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.310
I shouldn't say boot, but they
start in a matter of seconds.

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:23.940
And as long as your process
needs to start taking traffic,

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:27.070
that's all it takes
for it to do that.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:31.030
So let's quickly demo one.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:34.020
Just see how the
containers look running

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:38.590
from the host point of view as
well as the container itself.

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.250
So here I have a
Google Cloud VM.

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:43.260
And I'm going to run
a container image

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:47.310
that I've created and pushed to
one of the container registries

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:47.860
that we have.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.277
So I'm going to
use the docker run

00:11:49.277 --> 00:11:52.140
command to run this container.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:54.520
And once I run
that, it has started

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.023
that container over here.

00:11:56.023 --> 00:11:57.690
JAMES DUKE: Did you
see how fast he was?

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:57.960
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:58.880
JAMES DUKE: Did you
see how fast he was?

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.130
No kernel boot time.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.000
SHAHID MASUD: So since I had
downloaded that image earlier

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.680
on this VM, it didn't even take
time to sort of start that.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:14.100
So if I look at what
images are running,

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:15.750
this is the image
that's been running,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.480
and it's been up 18 seconds
since I just started it.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.460
And so let's look
at what the process

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:26.460
tree looks like on the VM.

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.245
So as you can see,
this is the VM.

00:12:28.245 --> 00:12:29.370
So I'm looking at the host.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.780
I'm looking at all the
processes that are running.

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.350
So this is everything
that's running on the host.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.960
And part of that
tree, you will see,

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:42.180
is the containerd, which
is our container runtime,

00:12:42.180 --> 00:12:45.750
and the nginx process that I
just started as a container.

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.730
So you'll see, this
is just one set

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.010
of processes within the
other processes that

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.670
are running on that host.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:55.140
So there's nothing special
from the host point of view

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:58.320
for these processes, other
than it's just grouped together

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.890
and it has certain boundaries
associated with that.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:05.580
Next what I can do is I can
look inside that container,

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:10.440
and I can attach myself
to that container,

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.690
and see what the
process tree looks

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.110
like from within that container.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:16.890
So what I can do is I
can start another command

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:21.220
inside that container by
using the exec command.

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.860
So you see the command
prompt has changed here,

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.290
and now I am in the
context of that container.

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:28.800
This may look very
similar to a VM.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.100
When you log into a VM,
you have a certain command

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:32.970
prompt and so on.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:38.580
So if I look at the process
tree within the container,

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:41.640
you'll see it's a very
limited set of processes.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.950
It doesn't even see
the containerd process,

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:46.050
that's the parent or
the container shim.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:49.180
It only sees the nxginx
processes that I started.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:50.820
And since I'm running
that ps command

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.180
and I just started
the [INAUDIBLE] shell,

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:54.430
you see that as well.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:56.190
So from the container
point of view,

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.602
it has the whole host
available to itself.

00:13:58.602 --> 00:14:00.060
It doesn't see any
other processes.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.740
It cannot interact with
any other processes.

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.770
So that's where that
isolation comes in.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.390
But from the point
of view of the host,

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:08.820
you can see pretty
much everything

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.720
that's running on the host.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.420
So that's what
the difference is.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:13.500
There's no magic here.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:16.630
It's just processes that
have been sort of put

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:19.543
in the sandbox or a container.

00:14:19.543 --> 00:14:21.460
SHAHID MASUD: In actual
fact, the Linux kernel

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:23.660
has no concept of a container.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.140
It's a purely user
space construct.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:30.160
So it's just built up of
processes, the sandbox,

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:31.685
from one another.

00:14:31.685 --> 00:14:33.310
The reason we're
going into this detail

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:35.838
is because, as you can
see, Shahid right there

00:14:35.838 --> 00:14:37.463
has-- do you have
root on this machine?

00:14:37.463 --> 00:14:39.558
Do you have root here?

00:14:39.558 --> 00:14:40.600
SHAHID MASUD: I might be.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:42.282
I'm not sure.

00:14:42.282 --> 00:14:44.530
JAMES DUKE: Shaid can look
inside all of that process

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:45.125
tree.

00:14:45.125 --> 00:14:47.620
That is to say, if you
have sufficient permissions

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:50.850
on the host, then any containers
that are running in that

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:52.930
are potentially visible to you.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.540
So when we talk about
security, and we

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:57.100
talk about how to
secure a cluster

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:58.820
and how to run
secure clusters, it's

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:00.070
not just about the containers.

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:01.653
It's not just about
the names, places.

00:15:01.653 --> 00:15:04.940
It's also about the operating
system level access as well.

00:15:04.940 --> 00:15:05.940
SHAHID MASUD: All right.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:07.350
We can switch back
to the slides.

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:14.200
So let's look at some
of the other terminology

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:15.260
that we talked about.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:16.690
So what are microservices?

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:20.440
Again, the things you see
associated with microservices

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.330
are that they're
loosely coupled,

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:26.050
they're sort of fine-grained,
they provide some minimal set

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:31.930
of functionality that's useful,
and they make you more agile,

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:34.610
and they should be
testable on their own,

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:36.175
and so on and so forth.

00:15:36.175 --> 00:15:39.620
JAMES DUKE: Who's built
or deployed microservices?

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:40.470
OK.

00:15:40.470 --> 00:15:42.100
About a quarter, I would say.

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:44.110
OK, OK.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:47.802
So for those of you who you've
done it, what was the thinking?

00:15:47.802 --> 00:15:49.010
What was the reasoning there?

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:49.760
Someone shout out.

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.040
Why go microservices?

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.310
AUDIENCE: Extract business
logic from your monolith.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.560
JAMES DUKE: Extract business
logic from your monolith.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:56.100
Excellent.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:56.600
Excellent.

00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:57.804
Have we got one more?

00:15:57.804 --> 00:16:00.650
AUDIENCE: Break up the monolith
to improve the scalabilitiy.

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:01.370
JAMES DUKE: Break
up the monolith

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:02.600
to improve scalability.

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:03.770
Also excellent.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.720
All of these things,
they are absolutely

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:07.273
the value of the microservices.

00:16:07.273 --> 00:16:08.690
So let's dig into
the two of those

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.945
and use those to
explain the buzzword.

00:16:10.945 --> 00:16:15.130
So separate the business
logic from the monolith.

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:17.630
In a moment, we'll talk about
why you use these things.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:22.000
But the short version of
that is these are tools.

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.517
And tools should be used
to solve business problems.

00:16:24.517 --> 00:16:26.350
Technology just by
itself, while very cool--

00:16:26.350 --> 00:16:28.300
and I'd say it's obviously
a passion of mine--

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:29.800
it doesn't solve
real-world problems

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.540
by being cool technology.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:34.630
It solves real-world problems by
being applied in the right way

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.300
to real-world problems.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:39.040
And so to the point of
extracting the business logic,

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:41.100
having everything
tied up in one thing--

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:42.670
it makes it very
difficult for you

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:44.620
to focus on the things that
are important for your business

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.060
or for your
customers' businesses,

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:47.800
because there's so
much other stuff

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:50.950
wrapped around it in this
giant, sprawling, in some cases

00:16:50.950 --> 00:16:52.990
decades-old piece of software.

00:16:52.990 --> 00:16:57.370
And it becomes very difficult
to say, OK, well, it's now 2019.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:00.223
I need to do some stuff
because the market's changed.

00:17:00.223 --> 00:17:01.390
My competitors have changed.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:02.660
I have a new idea.

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.702
And it makes it very
difficult to do that when it

00:17:04.702 --> 00:17:06.329
comes with all the other craft.

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:09.650
And the same is true of
the scaling piece, right?

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:11.660
If I have a monolith,
and I suddenly

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:14.510
have the success-disaster
scenario I described earlier,

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:16.940
I get 100 or 1,000
decks' traffic

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.670
that I do on a usual
basis, to scale

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:24.380
that, I have to either absorb
it in the existing monolith

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:27.329
or recreate instances
of the monolith

00:17:27.329 --> 00:17:29.830
and manage traffic
between those instances.

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:32.330
And that is a very, very-- well,
can be a very, very painful

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.730
thing to do, because often
those monoliths are not

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:36.290
designed to spin up quickly.

00:17:36.290 --> 00:17:37.790
They have a bunch
of dependencies.

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:39.830
In some cases, they run
on specific hardware.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.832
There's all sorts of reasons why
that can become very difficult.

00:17:42.832 --> 00:17:44.790
But if you break each
part of that monolith out

00:17:44.790 --> 00:17:47.330
into its own
microservice, now I can

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:50.460
scale each one of those
services independently.

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:52.190
I can modify and update
each one of those

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.200
independently to change
the business logic,

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:56.630
to add new features.

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.180
In summary, it
makes us more agile,

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.090
because it allows us to respond
to changing market conditions

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:04.778
and, indeed, changing
technology conditions as well.

00:18:04.778 --> 00:18:06.570
SHAHID MASUD: And
testing-- the same thing.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:08.410
Once you have a smaller
piece of functionality

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:09.933
that you have to
test, your releases

00:18:09.933 --> 00:18:11.350
are quicker because
you don't have

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.600
to worry about testing
all the features

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:15.880
that you've built into
this big monolith.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:19.210
So I hope with these you have
a better understanding of why

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:24.150
the industry has been moving
towards microservices.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:25.740
Next, let's look at
the cloud native.

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:29.100
So cloud native means
built for the cloud

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.560
or sort of born in the
cloud, if you will.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:34.140
And typically, you
hear that if you

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:36.540
want to build for the cloud,
you have to use containers.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.200
You have to be a
microservices architecture.

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.250
And what it will give
you is quicker releases

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:42.990
and scalability and all that.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.305
Some of these reasons
we saw earlier.

00:18:45.305 --> 00:18:46.680
When we looked at
containers, you

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.430
saw that containers are
easy to sort of deploy

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:51.630
from one place to the other.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.510
You don't have to prepare
the infrastructure as much

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:57.270
to run your container because
you're bringing all the bits

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:58.680
that you need with you.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.000
So you're building the bits.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.070
And the infrastructure
is just providing

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.640
the compute and other
resources that you need.

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.250
JAMES DUKE: But it is important
to note the three things are

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:09.490
different, right?

00:19:09.490 --> 00:19:11.490
A containerized application
is not necessarily

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.305
the same as a
microservices architecture.

00:19:13.305 --> 00:19:14.430
They often go hand in hand.

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:15.858
But it's not necessarily true.

00:19:15.858 --> 00:19:17.400
And you could do
both of those things

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:18.720
and still not be cloud native.

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:21.240
Or you could be cloud native
and do neither of those things.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.280
The three are not
necessarily the same.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.470
We do typically see
them coming together.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:29.010
But certainly, from what
we've seen with our customers

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:33.180
and in the broader IT
and computing industry,

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.330
we have seen typically a
cloud-native application

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:38.340
display as a microservices
architecture.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:40.475
And it's usually
containerized, especially when

00:19:40.475 --> 00:19:41.850
we look at something
like Anthos,

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:46.440
all right, which naturally leads
towards a containerized and

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.840
microservices model.

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.150
And all of the three
things together

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:53.796
is where we kind of see the
future of our field going.

00:19:53.796 --> 00:19:56.230
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:19:56.230 --> 00:19:57.690
JAMES DUKE: So that's what.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:58.930
Now why.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.880
So we touched on
this a little bit.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:02.760
Shahid, why should
we use containers?

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.460
SHAHID MASUD: So I see
containers, and microservices,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.650
and all these as
enabling technologies.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.860
So they do not bring necessarily
benefit in themselves.

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:15.210
They enable you to sort
of reap the benefits

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.420
of the modern infrastructure
that's available in the cloud.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:22.110
So that's how you
should think about it.

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:23.560
This is not the end, in itself.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:25.150
It's a means to an end.

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:29.152
And you get all of the
benefits that we talked about

00:20:29.152 --> 00:20:30.360
that you get from containers.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:34.950
You get the portability,
you know, the scaling up

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:37.560
and down-- fast scaling up and
down and portability and all

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:38.640
that-- with that.

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:40.790
And it opens up a lot
of options for you.

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:44.100
You can run the same
container in a VM.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:47.550
You can run it in a
platform, like Kubernetes.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:51.480
Or you can run it even in
serverless platforms like Cloud

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:53.770
Run and so on.

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.340
And similarly,
microservices and containers

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:01.080
together provide some of the
benefits that we talked about.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.290
You can scale up your
application quicker.

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:07.800
But again, your application
has to be built in such a way

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:10.240
still that it can
be scaled up easily.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:12.240
And we'll see that
when we look at how,

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:15.270
as to how to think about
that when you're designing

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:17.670
your application, or
as you're converting

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.340
your monolith into
a microservices

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.645
or a modern architecture.

00:21:21.645 --> 00:21:23.895
JAMES DUKE: We do want to
talk a little bit about what

00:21:23.895 --> 00:21:26.820
the options are for where
we can run these containers.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.170
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:30.600
And quicker release
cycles are basically

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:35.280
since it's not a big application
that you're releasing,

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.270
your microservices are smaller.

00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:39.833
So it's easier to
manage the releases.

00:21:39.833 --> 00:21:42.000
If you think about it, when
you start off a project,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:45.090
it's easy to sort of
iterate on the code.

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:48.000
As the code base grows bigger,
than it becomes challenging.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.180
So keeping it in microservices
forces you sort of

00:21:51.180 --> 00:21:54.600
to keep that code base smaller,
as you're separating out

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.880
your business logic
into individual repos

00:21:56.880 --> 00:21:59.440
and individual release cycles.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.430
So what that gives
you is that, once you

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:05.760
have the image, once you've
built the image the right way,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:09.240
you can now run it in
many different types

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:10.770
of infrastructure.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:12.960
And here's some
examples, with just once

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.790
you've built your image--

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.750
just hello-world
in this example--

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:19.650
with one command,
you can run it.

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.730
You can pick the
runtime that you want.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:25.530
And you can deploy the same
image in any of these products

00:22:25.530 --> 00:22:26.947
that Google Cloud
Platform offers.

00:22:26.947 --> 00:22:28.613
JAMES DUKE: Yeah, so
very simple, right?

00:22:28.613 --> 00:22:29.730
Four different commands.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.020
You have the same
container running

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:32.530
in four different
infrastructures, or four

00:22:32.530 --> 00:22:34.010
different platforms,
should I say.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:35.880
And so when we talk
about portability, like,

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.130
this is what it looks
like in practice, right?

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:41.520
Now, in reality this is a little
bit of a trite example, right,

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:44.100
because most people aren't
pushing a single container

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.017
with a single command.

00:22:45.017 --> 00:22:46.767
More than likely, it
would be in some kind

00:22:46.767 --> 00:22:48.060
of orchestration platform.

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:50.430
But the same thing holds
true across the different

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.362
orchestration platforms, too.

00:22:52.362 --> 00:22:54.610
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah, so
here, you can look at--

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:57.100
you can't just at
random pick one day--

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:59.783
oh, I'm going to run my app
in App Engine or Cloud Run.

00:22:59.783 --> 00:23:00.950
JAMES DUKE: Well, you could.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:01.520
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah, you could.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:02.350
JAMES DUKE: I wouldn't
recommend that.

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:03.440
But you could.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:06.800
SHAHID MASUD: You can once
you have the container built.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:12.350
But here's some guidance on how
you can pick the right runtime.

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.020
And the way to look at it
is it's sort of a continuum.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:18.950
On one end, you have
pure infrastructure

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:21.650
that gives you a lot of
flexibility of how you want

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.690
to manage that infrastructure.

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:26.120
For example, VMs--
you can pick the image

00:23:26.120 --> 00:23:28.190
that you want to
deploy on your VM.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:30.120
You can configure it
the way you want to.

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.840
You can put libraries
that you want to on it,

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:35.420
and then you can run
your container on it.

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:36.860
And that's one option.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:39.710
And what you're getting
is a lot of control.

00:23:39.710 --> 00:23:44.000
What you're giving up
is, or what you're, I

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:45.710
guess, signing up
for, I should say,

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:47.960
is now you have to
maintain that VM.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.510
Since you created
a custom VM, you

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:53.420
have to make sure that any
updates that are needed

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.800
are applied to that.

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:58.070
And as you move in the
stack to the right,

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.800
you start giving
up that control.

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:05.300
You're letting the platform
or your cloud-hosting provider

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:07.130
take on some of those
responsibilities

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:11.360
of operational aspects
of your infrastructure.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:15.500
And you're focusing more and
more on just your workload.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.560
But your workload has
to conform to sort

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.650
of the APIs or standards
that that platform expects

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:22.580
it to have.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:26.340
So that's the trade-off
involved in moving

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.830
from left to the right of this.

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:31.960
And eventually, sort of
the new sort of nirvana

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:34.170
is serverless, where
you're not worried about

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:36.060
your infrastructure at all.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:39.540
And you're letting
your cloud provider

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:42.780
provide the infrastructure for
you based on your workload.

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.060
And all you're
providing is, again,

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.910
your image, which is exactly
the bit that you need,

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:50.220
the software that you
need, and nothing extra.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:53.730
And you're letting your hosting
provider provide the underlying

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:54.810
infrastructure for you.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:55.900
JAMES DUKE: Absolutely.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:57.440
OK, we've teased it enough.

00:24:57.440 --> 00:24:59.690
We've done all the background,
done the level setting.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:02.790
Let's jump into how we might
actually do this, right,

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:07.310
because it seems
like a daunting task.

00:25:07.310 --> 00:25:08.778
You're faced with
a giant monolith.

00:25:08.778 --> 00:25:10.320
Like I said, it
could be decades old.

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:12.350
It's a daunting,
daunting task to take on

00:25:12.350 --> 00:25:15.140
and say, OK, I want to
actually change this.

00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:16.530
Where do I start?

00:25:16.530 --> 00:25:17.315
So we've kind of--

00:25:17.315 --> 00:25:19.440
well, I mean, we didn't
come up with this approach.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.910
It's been around for a while.

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:23.347
But we wanted to share
with you some things

00:25:23.347 --> 00:25:24.930
today that we've
found to be effective

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:26.790
when we've done this
with our own customers,

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:29.250
and as a suggestion for
ways that you folks could

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.730
do the same.

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:33.720
So first step-- you need a list.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:35.850
You need to know
what do you have?

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:37.060
What does it look like?

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:38.020
What is it running?

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:39.490
What does it do?

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:41.250
What technologies
does it involve?

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.900
What are its dependencies?

00:25:42.900 --> 00:25:47.010
And more to the point,
what group is it in?

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:50.802
So classifying it on the
basis of a number of things.

00:25:50.802 --> 00:25:53.010
So we kind of came up with
these three groups, right?

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:53.827
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:25:53.827 --> 00:25:56.160
JAMES DUKE: So we start off
with stateless applications.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.380
As you might imagine,
stateless applications

00:25:58.380 --> 00:26:01.070
tend to be the easiest
group to deal with.

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:03.940
And so we tend to deal with them
first, things like web front

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:06.270
ends, existing services--

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:08.090
maybe they're micro,
maybe they're not--

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:10.770
and existing APIs that
just take in data,

00:26:10.770 --> 00:26:13.320
transform, and send
out that data, anything

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:15.593
that has no statement,
anything that takes a request,

00:26:15.593 --> 00:26:17.010
gives a response,
but doesn't rely

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.783
on some kind of
underlying state.

00:26:19.783 --> 00:26:21.200
Group all those
together, and they

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:22.550
tend to be our first movers.

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:25.030
And that tends to be
our first place to go.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:27.020
Web front ends in
particular seem

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:29.370
to be a very popular approach.

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:33.610
Part of the reason for this
is you want this first mover

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:37.230
to be non-trivial.

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:39.882
You don't necessarily want it
to be the number one revenue

00:26:39.882 --> 00:26:42.090
generator for the business
because the first time you

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:44.183
do this, there's a bit
of a learning curve.

00:26:44.183 --> 00:26:46.350
But you do want it to be
something that's meaningful

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:48.720
and that has some kind
of real-world use,

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:51.930
because if you just start
with a hello-world application

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:55.350
that nobody uses, it doesn't
really prove anything out.

00:26:55.350 --> 00:26:57.150
So often we'll find
that web front ends,

00:26:57.150 --> 00:26:59.670
whilst they are in the
critical path for a lot of user

00:26:59.670 --> 00:27:01.680
journeys, they tend not to be--

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.840
well, hopefully-- they tend
not to be too complicated

00:27:04.840 --> 00:27:07.680
and are stateless, usually,
and so lend themselves

00:27:07.680 --> 00:27:09.550
very well to this first group.

00:27:09.550 --> 00:27:10.370
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:14.430
JAMES DUKE: Then the second
group, temporary state.

00:27:14.430 --> 00:27:16.222
What do we mean by
temporary state, Shahid?

00:27:16.222 --> 00:27:17.680
SHAHID MASUD: So
temporary state is

00:27:17.680 --> 00:27:19.770
something where an
application, when it starts up,

00:27:19.770 --> 00:27:21.750
it starts building up its state.

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.980
It doesn't sort of persist
that state between startups.

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:29.220
And a typical example is
a caching application--

00:27:29.220 --> 00:27:31.170
Redis or memcache or
anything like that.

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:33.130
When it starts up, it
doesn't have any state.

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:37.560
But as people put items in that
cache, it starts building up.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:41.040
And if you had to restart that,
it's not a catastrophic event.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.300
You could restart it.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.480
But you'll suffer some
loss in response time.

00:27:45.480 --> 00:27:47.880
Or there might be
some errors that you

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:50.010
might see as it's restarting.

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:53.910
Similarly, applications that
sort of take on different role

00:27:53.910 --> 00:27:54.970
once they start up.

00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:57.330
For example, you could
have a master and a slave.

00:27:57.330 --> 00:27:59.470
When they start up,
they are all the same.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.262
But then they somehow
coordinate to pick one master--

00:28:02.262 --> 00:28:02.970
things like that.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:03.880
Redis has that.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:06.070
And there are other
examples of that as well.

00:28:06.070 --> 00:28:09.630
So this is sort of the second
group that we've come up with.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:11.790
And we're calling
it temporary state,

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:15.430
because again, that state is
not persisted across restarts.

00:28:15.430 --> 00:28:19.070
It's something that it
acquires as it's running.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:25.070
So these can be great second
movers, because again, once you

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.840
have the knowledge of what does
it take to run applications

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.440
as containers in a more
dynamic environment,

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:34.400
you are ready to take on the
challenging of how to build

00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:35.870
that state dynamically.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:40.220
How can you elect a
master when the existing

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:43.760
master dies without someone
manually intervening and so on?

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:47.750
Or how can you build up maybe
the cache from other replicas

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:51.250
and sort of redistribute
cache as one of the replicas

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:52.680
dies or something like that?

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:55.550
So that's why this is our second
group that you should look at.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:57.740
JAMES DUKE: And then
thirdly and finally,

00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:00.620
stateful applications--
databases,

00:29:00.620 --> 00:29:03.840
file servers, and raw storage.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:05.960
It's not impossible-- in
fact, it's very doable--

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:10.190
to move stateful applications
into a containerized or

00:29:10.190 --> 00:29:12.530
microservices architecture.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:16.160
But it's more complicated
than the prior ones.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:19.680
Typically, many, especially
more legacy databases,

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:23.420
aren't necessarily set
up to just add more nodes

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:25.970
to get more capacity, right?

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.630
They have a concept
of master and slave

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:31.040
but in a very different way.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:34.877
They're often not dynamically
deciding and voting

00:29:34.877 --> 00:29:35.960
and promoting new masters.

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:39.200
Some do, but many don't.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.360
And so these really--

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:43.850
these probably want
to be the last thing

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:47.930
that you would consider, largely
because they benefit the least.

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:51.980
They benefit the least from
migrating to this new paradigm.

00:29:51.980 --> 00:29:59.000
And they have the least amount
of benefit for most complexity

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:00.585
to actually change them as well.

00:30:00.585 --> 00:30:01.960
SHAHID MASUD: And
how many of you

00:30:01.960 --> 00:30:04.760
have tried scaling MySQL
database, for example?

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:07.000
You can't just add
a replica and expect

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:08.470
it to work out of the box.

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:13.775
So it requires some sort of
manual design and decisions

00:30:13.775 --> 00:30:15.400
that you have to do
that you can't just

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:17.500
say, oh, I need
another MySQL instance,

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:19.820
and just add it there,
and go from there.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:24.730
So that's why these are more
difficult to containerize.

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:26.980
And the benefit
isn't as much there,

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:31.367
because you won't be scaling
these up and down dynamically

00:30:31.367 --> 00:30:31.950
to begin with.

00:30:34.880 --> 00:30:37.500
JAMES DUKE: So first
step, list and group.

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:41.400
Second step, containerize
parts of the application.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:45.310
In most cases, you probably
don't want to go-- well,

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.980
it's possible to go
to a big-bang approach

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:49.992
to all of this and say,
hey, you know what?

00:30:49.992 --> 00:30:51.700
We're going to take
our whole company out

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:54.902
of our regular release schedule
for six weeks or three weeks.

00:30:54.902 --> 00:30:57.110
And we're going to go, like,
just do the whole thing.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:30:58.860
We're going to take
the whole application,

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.420
wrap it up in containers,
and release it out.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:02.470
Lovely in practice.

00:31:02.470 --> 00:31:05.680
Very, very, very difficult to
do in-- sorry, lovely in theory.

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:07.600
Very, very difficult
to do in practice.

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:10.600
In practice, in most cases,
most real-world scenarios,

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:13.340
you'll want to do this
one step at a time.

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:15.460
So once you have your
list, and your groupings,

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:16.960
and your priorities,
then it becomes

00:31:16.960 --> 00:31:18.585
about finding parts
of that application

00:31:18.585 --> 00:31:20.570
to move to those
containers first.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:21.570
SHAHID MASUD: All right.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:23.820
JAMES DUKE: So some examples.

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:27.350
The most basic example of all
of this is take the monolith,

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:29.830
and put it into a
container, right?

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:31.080
It's the fastest way.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:33.780
And technically, you now have a
containerized workload, right?

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:34.770
Check the box.

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:36.240
Yes, I can do containers.

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:38.490
It's quick to deploy
and actually can

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:40.562
be automated, which we'll
come to in a second.

00:31:40.562 --> 00:31:42.270
This allows you to
prove out the concept.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:44.660
It allows you to get
comfortable with the technology

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.650
and, depending on the
nature of the monolith,

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:50.370
may allow some scaling that
wasn't possible before.

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.950
But you don't really
get the full benefit.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:55.950
Having said that, it can be
a very worthwhile exercise,

00:31:55.950 --> 00:31:57.690
both from a learning
perspective,

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:01.460
but also from a "need to
move right now" perspective.

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:04.470
And, you know, the reality
is many times in the projects

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:07.230
that we've seen, there is some
kind of compelling event--

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:11.810
Black Friday, Cyber Monday,
a holiday, a sports event,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:15.030
or even something as mundane as
a data center lease expiring.

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:17.370
I have to get out my
data center by April 29,

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:19.480
or whatever it might be.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:24.400
SHAHID MASUD: So this is part of
the lift-and-shift sort of kind

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:27.020
of migration, where you're
lifting the application,

00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:30.630
just containerizing
it, and shifting it

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.190
as a container to the cloud.

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:38.040
And there are ways to
do this automatically.

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:42.360
In fact, if you saw the
keynote this morning,

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:48.030
you saw an example of an
app being migrated from a VM

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:50.460
into Kubernetes Engine
running as containers.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:52.980
So there's Anthos
Migrate, which helps

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:54.310
you do this automatically.

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:56.240
So you can do it
manually as well.

00:32:56.240 --> 00:32:58.470
But there are instances
where you can just

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:01.510
use the automated
service like Anthos

00:33:01.510 --> 00:33:04.060
to do it for you as well.

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:07.407
So again, this can be a
good start to your journey.

00:33:07.407 --> 00:33:08.990
JAMES DUKE: So the
next approach would

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:11.450
be, OK, so let's
think about how we

00:33:11.450 --> 00:33:15.530
would migrate some parts of a
three-tier application, right?

00:33:15.530 --> 00:33:19.780
So in many cases, the
way you would do this,

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.555
you would put this behind
a load balancer, right?

00:33:22.555 --> 00:33:23.930
Put a load-balancer
in front of--

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:25.420
you may already have one.

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:27.907
But put one behind the
Google Cloud Load Balancer.

00:33:27.907 --> 00:33:30.490
And then we can look at the app
front end and the app backend.

00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:33.032
Remember, we said these are the
pieces that don't necessarily

00:33:33.032 --> 00:33:34.000
store state.

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:37.520
Maybe the backend might be one
of our semistate applications.

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:40.390
The front end would
typically be no state.

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:42.970
And then what we can do is
wrap those up in containers.

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:45.830
And now we can scale
those tiers individually.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:48.550
So if we have that
success-disaster scenario,

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:50.590
I can rapidly scale
up my front end

00:33:50.590 --> 00:33:54.160
to handle those requests
without necessarily having to do

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:56.710
the same scaling to my backend.

00:33:56.710 --> 00:33:59.235
Now, of course, you may
end up with contention

00:33:59.235 --> 00:34:00.610
to the database
in that scenario.

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:03.700
But at least it allows you to
respond-- at the very least,

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:05.890
allows you to respond with
a, we're working on it,

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:07.960
we'll get back to you,
instead of just returning

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:10.593
a 404 or a 503.

00:34:10.593 --> 00:34:12.010
SHAHID MASUD: One
thing to clarify

00:34:12.010 --> 00:34:16.250
is that red box does not
show one container boundary.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:18.429
The white boxes are
really containers.

00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:20.560
So you want to run the
front end separately

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:23.260
in a separate container
and the backend separately.

00:34:23.260 --> 00:34:26.300
And this is a nice transition
from a monolith as well.

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:29.260
This could be the first step
at breaking up a monolith.

00:34:29.260 --> 00:34:33.580
Typically, sort of the
front end part of a monolith

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.150
is easy to break up.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:36.670
So you can start with that.

00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:39.000
And you can separate
that out, modernize

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:42.969
it using some new
technology, and leave

00:34:42.969 --> 00:34:45.639
the rest of the monolith
running as the backend

00:34:45.639 --> 00:34:47.060
for that front end.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:49.550
So this could be a good
starting point on your journey.

00:34:49.550 --> 00:34:50.889
JAMES DUKE: But we're still
not all the way there, right?

00:34:50.889 --> 00:34:52.670
We still don't get
all the benefits.

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:55.449
So what can we go to next
where we get a little bit more

00:34:55.449 --> 00:34:58.620
of the benefits we
were talking about?

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.550
So we can start to think about
a transitional microservices

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:02.280
architecture.

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:06.490
We're still kind of mostly
in a monolith mode here.

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:09.630
But we're kind of dipping our
toes in the water a little bit.

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:14.700
We're going to say, well, OK,
one small part of that monolith

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:17.677
we're going to break out and
build into a separate service.

00:35:17.677 --> 00:35:20.010
And in some cases, this might
actually be completely new

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:20.670
functionality.

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:21.780
We've actually seen
both work, where

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:23.400
we've had customers
that have built

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:27.840
a new function or a new feature
as a new microservice and left

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:29.173
everything else in the monolith.

00:35:29.173 --> 00:35:31.215
We've also seen folks
who've said, well, actually

00:35:31.215 --> 00:35:31.830
you know what?

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:33.540
This one particular
part of my monolith,

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:36.480
I can carve that
out, and recreate

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:38.880
that same functionality,
that same feature,

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:41.160
in a separate service.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:43.498
Now, likely that means
you have dead code, right?

00:35:43.498 --> 00:35:45.540
There's probably code left
in that legacy backend

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:46.950
that now no longer operates.

00:35:46.950 --> 00:35:49.170
And that introduces
some problems, right?

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:50.760
You may have code duplication.

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:55.470
So maybe some technical deck to
remove that out later in time.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:57.660
It may be possible to
remove it there and then.

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.540
That's really very dependent
on the particular application

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:01.780
that we're working with.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:05.350
But the basic principle goes
take a piece out, move it

00:36:05.350 --> 00:36:06.345
over there.

00:36:06.345 --> 00:36:09.280
And now that piece
can scale very easily,

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:13.480
and we don't have to really
affect the existing monolith.

00:36:13.480 --> 00:36:16.000
There's very little
appetite for changing

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:19.070
the monolith in a lot of
the customers we work with,

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:21.700
especially things that are
in a more regulated industry

00:36:21.700 --> 00:36:24.247
or in a more traditional
enterprise-type space, where

00:36:24.247 --> 00:36:26.830
they don't really want to touch
it because it has been running

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:28.570
that way for 10
years, and they're

00:36:28.570 --> 00:36:31.240
very nervous about
changes breaking it.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:32.740
So when we do it
like this, we don't

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:34.180
have to touch that monolith.

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:36.640
We can leave it well alone,
so there's very little risk

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:37.418
to the business.

00:36:37.418 --> 00:36:38.210
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:40.090
And this is a great
way of reducing risk

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:42.850
as you're migrating,
because taking

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:46.418
small steps, making small
changes, reduces your risk.

00:36:46.418 --> 00:36:48.460
You don't have to worry
about making a big change

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:50.293
and upsetting something
that you didn't even

00:36:50.293 --> 00:36:53.750
know might go wrong when
you start peeling things.

00:36:53.750 --> 00:36:56.470
The other thing is, when
you take small steps,

00:36:56.470 --> 00:36:59.600
it's easier to sort of estimate
what you would need to do.

00:36:59.600 --> 00:37:02.525
And you can apply your
learnings to the next steps

00:37:02.525 --> 00:37:03.650
that you will take as well.

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:06.010
So the first time you're
building a microservice,

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:08.110
you might be learning a lot.

00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.732
And you can apply
those learnings

00:37:09.732 --> 00:37:11.440
as you build more and
more microservices.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:15.430
If you start with
four or five, you'll

00:37:15.430 --> 00:37:17.860
be repeating the same
mistakes across four or five

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:19.390
work streams that
you could avoid

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:22.650
by doing one change at a time.

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:24.060
JAMES DUKE: So
then the next step

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:27.090
is let's add an API gateway.

00:37:27.090 --> 00:37:30.330
And now the API gateway
becomes the arbiter

00:37:30.330 --> 00:37:32.580
of where all traffic goes.

00:37:32.580 --> 00:37:34.100
So any time a request comes in--

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:36.517
it can come directly from the
load balancer in some cases,

00:37:36.517 --> 00:37:39.240
or it can come from the
application front end--

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:42.270
now, the API gateway
decides where traffic goes.

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:44.460
So very minimal change as well.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:46.020
No changes required
to the front end,

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:49.190
except pointing at a different
IP address or service name.

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:52.820
And now the API gateway
gets to decide, OK,

00:37:52.820 --> 00:37:55.220
which backend do I need
to send this request to?

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:56.970
Is this something that
I need to send over

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:59.430
to the backend, the legacy
backend, the monolith,

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:00.930
or is this something
that I can send

00:38:00.930 --> 00:38:03.555
to one of these fancy new
microservices that we have?

00:38:03.555 --> 00:38:07.140
And this is particularly
powerful because--

00:38:07.140 --> 00:38:07.950
think about this.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:12.700
As you add service B,
C, D, E, and so on,

00:38:12.700 --> 00:38:14.460
now the only piece
that has to change

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:17.700
to support those new
services is the API gateway.

00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:19.630
So it becomes a single
point of configuration

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:22.140
that's basically
routing your traffic

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:25.720
from monolith or microservice.

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:27.820
SHAHID MASUD: So it gives
you that flexibility

00:38:27.820 --> 00:38:29.770
of where to send that traffic.

00:38:29.770 --> 00:38:32.770
And it hides that detail of
what microservices you actually

00:38:32.770 --> 00:38:35.890
have running behind the
scenes from your clients,

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:37.710
from the consumers
of those services.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:39.460
So they don't have to
know that now you've

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:42.070
migrated service B
or C over and you're

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:44.080
no longer calling the monolith.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:46.570
It can also provide
some added benefits.

00:38:46.570 --> 00:38:49.730
For example, if you
wanted to add TLS, or SSL,

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:51.970
or this [INAUDIBLE],,
like you wanted to secure

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:54.940
the communication, the
API gateway can typically,

00:38:54.940 --> 00:38:57.590
you can add that functionality
very easily using the API

00:38:57.590 --> 00:38:58.090
gateway--

00:38:58.090 --> 00:39:01.900
API gateway support
providing TLS.

00:39:01.900 --> 00:39:03.940
It can also provide
rate limiting.

00:39:03.940 --> 00:39:07.870
Or it can have other
features, like if it detects

00:39:07.870 --> 00:39:11.247
some failures and stuff, it
can provide sort of circuit

00:39:11.247 --> 00:39:12.330
breaking and other things.

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:15.080
So depending on the API
gateway of your choice,

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:17.830
it can add a lot of features
to all your services

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:18.850
with very little cost.

00:39:18.850 --> 00:39:22.620
All of these features are
built into the API gateway

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:23.480
that you might have.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:27.490
So you get all those
benefits with that.

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:29.950
JAMES DUKE: So
then finally, there

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:33.280
is the true microservices--
the end goal

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:35.120
that we've been
shooting for all along.

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:38.740
You'll see that the API gateway
is still a key component.

00:39:38.740 --> 00:39:41.350
Eventually, we could get to a
place where we don't need that.

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:43.517
You can have something like
a service mesh or Vistio

00:39:43.517 --> 00:39:46.000
or something like that to
control all the communications.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:50.020
But we still consider it a
true microservices place,

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:52.500
even though we have the
API gateway involved.

00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:54.313
And at this point,
we've basically

00:39:54.313 --> 00:39:56.230
taken the previous one
and started spinning up

00:39:56.230 --> 00:39:57.397
these extra services, right?

00:39:57.397 --> 00:40:01.510
Service B, service-- if you have
a service, B through Service N.

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:03.790
And now we've got
all that good stuff--

00:40:03.790 --> 00:40:05.230
everything we
talked about in why

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:07.690
microservices and
containerization and cloud

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:08.950
native design gets--

00:40:08.950 --> 00:40:10.930
why it gets our business.

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:13.660
We can scale every
service individually.

00:40:13.660 --> 00:40:15.610
Each one has its own lifecycle.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:19.000
So we've seen
customers who have gone

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:22.630
from releasing once a quarter
to releasing 60 or 100 times

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:25.390
a day, because now
they can release small,

00:40:25.390 --> 00:40:27.440
small things very,
very, very, very quickly

00:40:27.440 --> 00:40:29.740
in that pace of
innovation, because it

00:40:29.740 --> 00:40:33.850
improves so much, just by
moving to this type of model.

00:40:33.850 --> 00:40:35.543
The downside, of course--

00:40:35.543 --> 00:40:36.460
it's harder to manage.

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:37.300
SHAHID MASUD: Yes.

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:38.050
JAMES DUKE: Right?

00:40:38.050 --> 00:40:41.180
Or different to manage, because
if you're a company that

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:43.330
is used to dealing
with a monolith,

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:44.920
it requires a
change of thinking,

00:40:44.920 --> 00:40:46.937
because now I've got
multiple services

00:40:46.937 --> 00:40:49.270
with multiple teams that are
responsible for maintaining

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:50.542
those services.

00:40:50.542 --> 00:40:52.000
I probably need to
create some kind

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:55.180
of centralized CICD system,
some kind of configuration

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:58.240
management platform, all
these kind of overheads

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:01.970
which go into orchestrating
all of these microservices.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:06.180
But when this happens, when you
get to this beautiful state,

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:09.600
because you have so
much agility and ability

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.690
to innovate in the
application development teams,

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:15.180
the company or the business
is able to spend so much more

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:19.000
of its resources on the thing
that actually makes money,

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:21.030
because running the
perfect Kubernetes cluster

00:41:21.030 --> 00:41:21.940
doesn't make money.

00:41:21.940 --> 00:41:25.800
Well, I mean, maybe for Google,
but not for most customers,

00:41:25.800 --> 00:41:28.707
whereas being able to innovate
and being agile and respond

00:41:28.707 --> 00:41:31.040
to the market-- that's where
the true business value is.

00:41:31.040 --> 00:41:33.410
And this is the
way you get there.

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:36.990
Now, does anybody
recognize that pattern?

00:41:36.990 --> 00:41:39.810
Anyone know what it's called?

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:42.180
I wish I'd made this
up, but I didn't.

00:41:42.180 --> 00:41:44.730
This is the Strangler pattern.

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:48.027
So this is-- yeah,
[CHUCKLES] sounds

00:41:48.027 --> 00:41:49.110
a bit macabre, doesn't it?

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:52.800
It actually comes
from a tree analogy.

00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:55.050
And so the principle
goes like this.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.360
If you have a big, old,
well-established tree--

00:41:57.360 --> 00:41:58.210
we're in California.

00:41:58.210 --> 00:41:59.345
So let's call it a Redwood.

00:41:59.345 --> 00:42:04.010
You have a big Redwood tree,
which represents the monolith.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:06.210
And then you have a
bunch of vines growing up

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:08.540
around that tree.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:11.520
As those vines become more
and more mature and stronger

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:14.400
and stronger and
hardier and hardier,

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:16.500
there comes a point in
time at which they are now

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:20.010
providing more structural
integrity than the tree

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:21.900
around which they are growing.

00:42:21.900 --> 00:42:26.550
If that tree dies and rots
away, the structure remains.

00:42:26.550 --> 00:42:28.700
And that's basically what
we're trying to do here.

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:30.980
We are trying to
build microservices

00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:33.380
around the monoliths
such that we've

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:35.440
made the monolith obsolete.

00:42:35.440 --> 00:42:37.160
And we can shut it
down, and remove it,

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:40.431
and throw it away, and never
shed a tear for it ever again.

00:42:40.431 --> 00:42:42.820
And so that's the basic premise
of the Strangler pattern.

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:45.370
And it gives you
that flexibility.

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:49.748
And it gives you a gradual
or partial path forward,

00:42:49.748 --> 00:42:51.623
so you don't have to do
everything in one go.

00:42:51.623 --> 00:42:52.430
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah.

00:42:52.430 --> 00:42:56.150
And as we mentioned, your
organizational processes

00:42:56.150 --> 00:42:57.710
would need to change,
as well, as you

00:42:57.710 --> 00:42:59.920
move from one type of
architecture to the other.

00:42:59.920 --> 00:43:02.420
CICD is one thing we mentioned.

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:04.340
Just increasing
developer velocity

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:05.840
will have its own challenges.

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:07.730
When you have multiple
releases going out,

00:43:07.730 --> 00:43:10.940
imagine doing tens of releases
instead of one release

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:12.650
a quarter or whatever
you're used to.

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:16.430
So this allows you to
gradually change your posture

00:43:16.430 --> 00:43:19.220
from a traditional
organization to a more

00:43:19.220 --> 00:43:22.540
agile microservices-based
organization as well,

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:25.640
with less risk.

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:27.940
JAMES DUKE: All right,
finally, profit.

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:31.070
At the end of the day, that's
what business is all about,

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:32.000
right?

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:36.320
Three easy steps-- categorize,
migrate small parts,

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:40.510
build up to the final
thing, and profit.

00:43:40.510 --> 00:43:42.950
All right, so some
useful resources.

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:47.640
We are very blessed here to
have a wonderful set of labs.

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:50.120
Please go find the
hands-on lab session.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.340
You've got all sorts
of fancy machinery

00:43:52.340 --> 00:43:54.500
there to go try out
these particular labs.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:59.830
We highly recommend the GKE
Best Practices Qwiklabs Quest.

00:43:59.830 --> 00:44:02.595
Shahid and the team put a lot
of work into building those out.

00:44:02.595 --> 00:44:04.220
And they kind of
guide you through some

00:44:04.220 --> 00:44:05.650
of these best practices
we've talked about here.

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:06.830
SHAHID MASUD: Yeah,
And they guide you.

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:08.810
They are labs that
start from just building

00:44:08.810 --> 00:44:10.830
your first container
and deploying it,

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:14.690
to all the way to
hardening those containers,

00:44:14.690 --> 00:44:17.570
adding security or
cluster, as well as going

00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:20.330
all the way to maybe deploying
a service mesh, and so on and so

00:44:20.330 --> 00:44:20.830
forth.

